1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nge7 { In principle a logical move. Black reinforces his Nc6 and thus indirectly protects e5. Of course this move has downsides - most importantly blocking in the Be7. } 4. Nc3! { I think the best and the most principled move. White doesn't plan to play the slow maneuvering game the Ruy Lopez is famous for. Instead he opts for rapid piece play, which is most effective against the slow setup Black is planning to play. } 4... g6 (4... d6 5. d4 a6 (5... Bd7 6. O-O Ng6 7. Nd5 Be7 8. c3 O-O 9. Be2 $14) (5... exd4 6. Nxd4 Bd7 7. Be3 Nxd4 8. Bxd7+ Qxd7 9. Qxd4 $14) 6. Be2 exd4 7. Nxd4 Nxd4 8. Qxd4 Nc6 9. Qd3 $14 { Gives White a very pleasant position reminiscent of the Philidor Exchange variation (1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 d6 3.d4 exd4 4.Nxd4). These structures simply give a long lasting positional edge for White due to the eternal space advantage. }) 5. d4 { White opens the position as he is the one enjoying a lead in development. A typical strategy, which enhances the piece activity of the side enjoying better development. } 5... exd4 (5... Bg7 6. dxe5 Nxe5 7. Nxe5 Bxe5 8. Bh6! $14 { leaves Black with serious problems regarding his king safety. }) 6. Nd5 { The most testing. The knight is positionally untouchtable and puts a finger on many critical squares (f6, c7, e7). } (6. Nxd4 Bg7 7. Be3 O-O 8. Qd2 d5! $14 { is not too bad for Black, who frees his position. }) 6... Bg7 (6... Nxd5? 7. exd5 Qe7+ 8. Kf1! Ne5 9. Qxd4 $16 { is just very bad for Black. }) 7. Bg5 { Keeps up the pressure and poses practical problems for Black. Simply castling e.g. would lose on the spot. } 7... h6 (7... O-O?? 8. Bxc6 f6 (8... bxc6 9. Nxe7+ $18) 9. Nxe7+ Qxe7 10. Bd5+ Kh8 11. Bd2 $18 { And White won a piece. }) 8. Bf6 { Disgusting pressure. Castling fails again for the same reasons. Black has to take now, which is anything but pleasant. } 8... Bxf6 { Sad necessity. } (8... Kf8 9. Bxg7+ Kxg7 10. Bxc6 Nxc6 11. Nxd4 $16 { Gives White great central advantage, while the Kg7 looks silly. }) 9. Nxf6+ Kf8 { Amazingly, there are over 100 master games in this position. Yes, Black is not lost, but his position feels so passive and without potential. Strange. } 10. Qd2 { Of course White wants to castle queenside. Furthermore, Qd2 prepares to fight for the dark squares, which are weaker due to the absence of the Bg7. } 10... d5 { The only plausible way to justify the whole setup. White can bank an advantage though with: } (10... Ng8 11. Nxg8 Kxg8 12. O-O-O $14 { Followed by Nxd4 is just a great control position with good prospects to attack in the future with h4-h5. }) (10... a6 11. Bc4 $14) 11. Bxc6 Nxc6 12. Nxd5 Bf5 { This is absolutely forced. Else White just chops off the pawn on d4 and is a pawn up for nothing. Instead, Black tries to solve his problems tactically. } 13. Nxc7 (13. Qf4 Bxe4 14. Qxe4 Qd6 15. Nxc7 Qxc7 16. O-O-O Re8 17. Qh4 $14) 13... Qxc7 14. exf5 Re8+ 15. Kf1 $14 { Saw White superior in most games played in this position. Great names like GM Anish Giri or GM David Navara lead this position to victory and I am sure it indeed should favor the White side. } *


